  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThis afternoon, most of the active seabreeze activity will be across \nthe Victoria Crossroads, but still generally on the low end of the \nPoP spectrum (15-20%) after 1 PM. While heat index values will \ngenerally flirt at the 110F mark on and off with the peak heat of \nthe afternoon, I will hold off on issuing a Heat Advisory, but we \nstill encourage South Texans to continue to practice heat safety and \nchecking in on the vulnerable population. Hydrate, hydrate, hydrate!\n\nAll eyes across the western Gulf are on Invest 97L. As of this \nwriting, the National Hurricane Center is giving the area of low \npressure a high chance (>60%) chance of developing into at least a \ntropical depression before it moves onshore. Hurricane model \nguidance generally moves 97L toward the TX/LA border area with much \nof the shower/thunderstorm activity moving toward Southeast Texas. \nOf the global models, the Euro is the wetter solution advecting in \nPWATs in excess of 2.0\" toward the Houston area, but locally, the \nedge of these higher PWATs generally will be along the upper Coastal \nBend/Victoria Crossroads. That means whatever storms develop in \nthese areas have the potential for heavier rainfall rates, \nespecially if training occurs. For this reason, the WPC has placed \nthese areas under a Marginal Risk for flooding (level 1 of 4) \nTuesday.\n\nWhatever comes of this system, it will move onshore and continue \nmoving to the northwest into north-central Texas. Mid-level high \npressure will continue to dominate the overall mid/upper-level flow, \npositioning South Texas in a broad easterly flow, allowing more \ntropical-like moisture and weak disturbances to move across the \nwestern Gulf and increasing rain chances/seabreeze activity to more \nmoderate chances this weekend (20-40% chances). In between any \nrainfall activity, heat-related concerns persist.",
